 "name": "S13: Ch5, Formal review + compensation",
 "text_content": "Pages 399 to 418. Chapter 5: The formal review process, Compensation.\n\nS\u014fn output (draft): the review system, and S\u014fn's compensation architecture.\n\nHeaviest adaptation in the book. Her compensation chapter assumes salary bands, equity refresh grants, and an engineering ladder. S\u014fn runs an embedded 21.5% revenue share baked into menu price, never surfaced as a service charge line item. Almost none of her comp machinery transfers. What does transfer is the principle underneath it: compensation must be legible, defensible, and connected to a review process people trust. Build that.\n\nMandatory read before starting: whatever scorecard dimensions Session 6 set. The review rubric scores against the same dimensions or the system is incoherent.\n\nDo not generate figures. Compensation numbers come from Airtable (St. Elmo Dashboard V2, appKHeje63inr1fLG) or they do not appear.",
 "description": "Pages 399 to 418. Chapter 5: The formal review process, Compensation.\n\nS\u014fn output (draft): the review system, and S\u014fn's compensation architecture.\n\nHeaviest adaptation in the book. Her compensation chapter assumes salary bands, equity refresh grants, and an engineering ladder. S\u014fn runs an embedded 21.5% revenue share baked into menu price, never surfaced as a service charge line item. Almost none of her comp machinery transfers. What does transfer is the principle underneath it: compensation must be legible, defensible, and connected to a review process people trust. Build that.\n\nMandatory read before starting: whatever scorecard dimensions Session 6 set. The review rubric scores against the same dimensions or the system is incoherent.\n\nDo not generate figures. Compensation numbers come from Airtable (St. Elmo Dashboard V2, appKHeje63inr1fLG) or they do not appear.",
 "markdown_description": "**Pages 399 to 418.** Chapter 5: The formal review process, Compensation.\n\n**S\u014fn output (draft):** the review system, and S\u014fn's compensation architecture.\n\n**Heaviest adaptation in the book.** Her compensation chapter assumes salary bands, equity refresh grants, and an engineering ladder. S\u014fn runs an embedded 21.5% revenue share baked into menu price, never surfaced as a service charge line item. Almost none of her comp machinery transfers. What does transfer is the principle underneath it: compensation must be legible, defensible, and connected to a review process people trust. Build that.\n\n**Mandatory read before starting:** whatever scorecard dimensions Session 6 set. The review rubric scores against the same dimensions or the system is incoherent.\n\n**Do not generate figures.** Compensation numbers come from Airtable (St. Elmo Dashboard V2, `appKHeje63inr1fLG`) or they do not appear.",
